        "title": "Business Entities Comparison for Student Founders",
        "content": "When Rahul, Priya, and Amit thought of a smart campus delivery and hostel supply app during their college fest, their first confusing question was: *\"Bro, how do we register a legal business? Should we make an Instagram page, get an agreement on a ₹100 stamp paper, or directly open a Private Limited company?\"*\n\nUnder the Indian legal system, there are different legal structures (business entities) for running a commercial business. Selecting the wrong legal structure at the initial stage can expose you to heavy income tax burdens, mandatory annual compliance penalties, personal asset forfeiture, or outright rejection by institutional investors. Below, we are completely dissecting 5 primary business entities + Section 8 non-profit structure for student founders.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Analysis of the 5 Business Entities\n\n#### 1. Sole Proprietorship (Ekal Swamitva)\n- **What is it?** This is the most basic and informal business structure where the business and founder are legally the same person. If you are a solo founder, freelancing (cross-reference: Module 15), or running a small local pilot trial, proprietorship naturally starts.\n- **Government Registration:** No incorporation at Ministry of Corporate Affairs (MCA). Aadhaar-linked Udyam MSME Certificate or local Shop & Establishment License / GST registration serves as entity proof.\n- **Founder Liability:** **UNLIMITED!** There is no legal wall between business and founder. If your proprietorship firm sinks in ₹10 Lakh debt or vendor lawsuit, the court can recover money from your personal bicycle, laptop, smartphone, or personal savings bank account.\n- **Taxation:** Business profits are treated as the founder's personal individual income and taxed under individual Income Tax Slab Rates (up to 30% + surcharge).\n\n#### 2. Partnership Firm (Indian Partnership Act, 1932)\n- **What is it?** A business owned by 2 or more partners who share profits, losses, and liabilities as per a Partnership Deed.\n- **Registration:** Optional under Indian Partnership Act, but recommended. Registered at the Registrar of Firms.\n- **Liability:** **UNLIMITED** for all partners. Each partner is personally liable for the firm's debts.\n- **Taxation:** Partnership firm taxed at flat 30% + surcharge + 4% cess. Partners' share of profit is exempt from tax.\n\n#### 3. Limited Liability Partnership (LLP)\n- **What is it?** A hybrid structure combining partnership flexibility with limited liability protection.\n- **Registration:** Mandatory at MCA via RUN-LLP form. LLP Agreement required.\n- **Liability:** **LIMITED** to partner's agreed contribution. Personal assets protected.\n- **Taxation:** Flat 30% + surcharge + 4% cess on LLP income. No DDT (Dividend Distribution Tax).\n\n#### 4. Private Limited Company (Pvt Ltd)\n- **What is it?** The gold standard for startups planning to raise VC funding. Separate legal entity from shareholders.\n- **Registration:** Mandatory at MCA via SPICe+ form. MOA & AOA required.\n- **Liability:** **LIMITED** to unpaid share capital. Shareholders' personal assets fully protected.\n- **Taxation:** Flat 25% (for companies with turnover up to ₹400 Cr) + surcharge + 4% cess.\n- **VC Preferred:** Only Pvt Ltd companies can issue equity shares, ESOPs, and sign term sheets with institutional investors.\n\n#### 5. One Person Company (OPC)\n- **What is it?** A Pvt Ltd company for a single founder. Combines sole proprietorship convenience with Pvt Ltd limited liability.\n- **Registration:** SPICe+ form at MCA. Mustnominate a nominee director.\n- **Liability:** LIMITED to share capital.\n- **Taxation:** Same as Pvt Ltd company.\n\n#### 6. Section 8 Company (Non-Profit)\n- **What is it?** A non-profit entity for social enterprises. Cannot distribute dividends.\n- **Registration:** SPICe+ form at MCA with Section 8 license from Central Government.\n- **Tax benefits:** Eligible for 12A and 80G registration for tax exemptions on donations.",
